"""Phase 2.1 conversation batch F: deeper multi-turn chats (3-4 user turns) with realistic drift, doubt and refinement."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("general_conversation", "c21f")
d = B.d

d("drift", "I'm thinking about adopting a dog.", "That's exciting. A few things to think about first: time (daily walks, training), cost (food, vet, insurance), your living space and how long you'll be away from home. What's your situation like?", "I live in a flat, work from home mostly", "Working from home helps with companionship and toilet training. In a flat, consider breeds or adult dogs with moderate energy and low barking, and check your lease allows pets. Many shelters have adult dogs whose personalities are already known.", "do they bark a lot in flats", "It depends on the dog and training, not just the building. Bored, under-exercised or lonely dogs bark more. Daily exercise, mental games and gradual alone-time training reduce it. Tell neighbours before you get one, and ask the shelter about each dog's barking history.", "ok we'll visit a shelter this weekend", "Good plan. Bring everyone who'll live with the dog, ask about the dog's history and health, and spend time walking the dog before you decide. It's fine to leave without adopting if the match doesn't feel right.")
d("drift", "can you help me understand my electricity bill", "Sure. Look for these parts: the usage in kWh, the rate per kWh, any fixed daily or monthly charge, and taxes or fees. Usage times the rate, plus fixed charges, gives most of your bill. What do you see on yours?", "usage 412 kWh, rate 0.18, fixed 12", "Usage cost: 412 x 0.18 = 74.16. Add the fixed 12 and you get 86.16 before taxes or other fees. If your actual bill is higher, the difference is probably taxes, surcharges, or the rate varying by time of day.", "bill says 97.40", "The difference is 11.24, which is about 13% of 86.16, so a tax or surcharge like VAT is plausible. Look for lines labelled tax, levy or surcharge. If something's unlabelled, ask your supplier to itemise.")
d("drift", "Help me pick a laptop under $800", "Happy to. What will you mostly do on it (browsing, office work, programming, video editing, gaming), and do you care more about battery life, screen quality or power?", "mostly coding and lots of Chrome tabs", "Prioritise memory and a fast SSD: at least 16 GB of RAM and a 512 GB SSD, with a recent mid-range processor such as a Ryzen 5/7 or Intel Core i5/i7. A good keyboard and a 1080p screen matter for long coding sessions. Avoid 8 GB machines.", "mac or windows?", "Both work for coding. A MacBook Air gives excellent battery life and a Unix-like terminal, but a new one is near the top of that budget, so consider a refurbished model. Windows laptops at that price give more RAM and ports, and run Linux in WSL for development. If you target iOS apps, you need a Mac.", "i'll look at refurbished macs", "Reasonable. Buy from a seller with a return window and warranty, check battery cycle count, and make sure it's not locked to a previous owner's account. Prefer 16 GB if you can find it, since memory can't be upgraded later.")
d("drift", "I want to get better at chess. I'm about 900 rated.", "At 900, the biggest gains usually come from avoiding blunders. Before every move, check what the opponent's last move threatens and whether your piece is safe. Solve short tactics puzzles daily, and learn one simple opening for each colour.", "which openings", "As White, the Italian Game or London System is easy to learn. As Black against 1.e4, try the Caro-Kann or a solid ...e5 setup; against 1.d4, the Slav or ...d5 setups. The best choice is one whose ideas you understand, not whose moves you memorise.", "how many puzzles a day", "Around 10 to 20 minutes daily is fine: quality over quantity. Take your time and try to calculate the whole line before moving. Analyse your lost games afterwards for the first move where things went wrong.")
d("drift", "I want to learn to code but I'm 45. Is it too late?", "Not at all. Many people learn programming in their 40s and beyond. What matters is a clear goal and consistent practice. What would you like to build or do with it?", "maybe change careers eventually", "Then start with one language that fits your target: Python for data and automation, JavaScript for web. Build small projects, keep them on GitHub, and pair that with your existing professional experience, which can make you more valuable in a domain-specific role.", "how long until I can get a job", "Realistically, 9 to 18 months of steady part-time study is common for an entry-level role, but it varies with your hours, background and the job market. Entry-level markets can be competitive, so portfolios, networking and a bridge role in your current industry help.")
d("drift", "My toddler won't eat vegetables", "That's very common. Keep offering small portions without pressure; it can take 10 to 15 exposures before a child accepts a food. Let them see you eat them, and let them help with washing and stirring.", "he spits them out", "Spitting out is part of learning new textures, so stay calm. Try different preparations: roasted instead of boiled, raw sticks with a dip, or veg blended into sauces. Don't force or bribe, which can create stronger resistance.", "should I worry about nutrition", "If his overall diet includes some fruit, protein and grains, and he's growing normally, usually not. If he eats a very narrow set of foods, loses weight, or you're worried, speak to your paediatrician or health visitor.")
d("drift", "Why do I always feel tired in the afternoon?", "Common reasons include a natural dip in alertness after lunch, a big carbohydrate-heavy meal, poor night sleep, dehydration or little movement. How are you sleeping and what do you usually eat at lunch?", "6 hours sleep, sandwich and chips", "Six hours is on the low end for most adults, and a large carb-heavy lunch can intensify the dip. Try aiming for 7 to 8 hours, adding protein and vegetables to lunch, drinking water and taking a 10-minute walk after eating.", "if it doesn't get better?", "If you get adequate sleep and still feel exhausted for several weeks, see a doctor to check for things like anaemia, thyroid problems, sleep apnoea or low mood.")
d("drift", "i want to write a novel", "That's a great goal. Do you have an idea in mind, or are you still looking? And do you prefer to plan the story first or find it as you write?", "idea: a lighthouse keeper discovers the light is calling ships from another world", "Intriguing premise. The key questions: what does the keeper want, what happens if he ignores the light, and what's the cost of answering? Starting from one clear character desire and a ticking problem keeps the plot moving.", "i don't know the ending", "That's fine; many writers discover it as they go. Write the first act so the keeper has a choice to make, and let your later chapters push him toward it. You can also write several possible endings in a paragraph each and see which one feels earned.")
d("doubt", "Is it true that we only have five basic tastes?", "Traditionally: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami. Some researchers argue for additional candidates such as fat and calcium taste, but these are less established.", "I thought tongue maps were a thing", "The tongue map, with specific regions for each taste, is a myth that came from a misread 1901 paper. All tastes can be detected across the tongue, although sensitivity varies slightly.", "so why do people believe it?", "It was printed in textbooks for decades and is easy to remember and test with a diagram. Because the sensitivity differences exist but are tiny, the map seemed plausible to generations of teachers.")
d("doubt", "my friend says I should buy crypto. I'm skeptical", "Healthy to be skeptical. Crypto is volatile and can lose most of its value quickly, and scams are common. If you want exposure, only use money you can afford to lose, stick to well-known assets and avoid anyone who guarantees returns.", "he says he made 3x last year", "That may be true for him, but it's one outcome among many, and people tend to share wins and stay quiet about losses. Past returns don't predict future ones. Ask him how much he risked and what he'd do if it fell 70%.", "ok so what's a safer option", "Broad index funds, savings accounts or bonds with an emergency fund first. They're less exciting, but they're more predictable. If you still want some crypto, a small fixed share like 1 to 5% of your investments limits the damage.")
d("doubt", "Are you sure about that?", "Which answer do you mean? I can't see an earlier answer in this conversation. If you tell me the claim, I'll recheck it and flag how confident I am.")
d("doubt", "Is it OK to trust AI for medical advice?", "Use me for general information, like understanding a term or preparing questions for your doctor, but not for diagnosis or treatment decisions. I can be wrong, and I can't examine you or see your history.", "what if I can't afford a doctor", "That's a real constraint. Look at community clinics, sliding-scale or free clinics, nurse advice lines and pharmacists, who can often advise on minor issues at no charge. If you have severe symptoms like chest pain, trouble breathing or sudden weakness, seek emergency care regardless of cost.")
d("doubt", "I think the earth might be flat. Not joking.", "I'd be glad to look at why you think so. The evidence for a round Earth is wide-ranging: ships disappear hull-first over the horizon, the shadow of Earth on the Moon is round, different stars are visible from different latitudes, and time zones and flights work in a globe geometry. What made you start to doubt it?", "i can't see any curve from where I live", "That's expected. Earth is huge, with a radius of about 6,371 km, so any small stretch looks flat to the eye, like a field. The curve shows up at scale: from a high-altitude flight, in sunsets at different heights, or in the way a ship's hull vanishes first. A simple test: watch a distant ship go over the horizon with a telescope.")
d("expert_chat", "Can you critique this plan? Launch a SaaS to track gym attendance, $5/month, aimed at small gyms.", "Strengths: a clear niche, a simple price point and a definable customer. Concerns: $5 a month per gym is likely too low for a business customer, and competitors such as Mindbody already serve this. Consider pricing per member or at least $30 to $50 a gym, and find a sharper wedge, like a feature small gyms lack. Talk to 10 gym owners before building.", "what would a sharper wedge be", "Look at what small gym owners complain about: failed payments, no-shows for classes, or churn. A product that recovers failed payments automatically, for example, has an obvious ROI you can price on. Interview owners and ask what they lose money on.")
d("expert_chat", "What are common mistakes in writing a thesis statement?", "Typical ones: stating a fact rather than an arguable claim, being too broad (\"Social media affects society\"), being a vague opinion (\"X is interesting\"), or packing in too many ideas. A good thesis takes a position, can be supported with evidence and previews your reasoning.", "Can you improve this: 'Pollution is bad'", "Try: \"Because urban air pollution raises asthma rates among children, cities should restrict diesel vehicles in school zones.\" It takes a position, names a cause and effect, and suggests a specific policy.")
d("expert_chat", "I'm a teacher. How do I handle a student who's constantly disruptive?", "Start by understanding the cause: attention-seeking, boredom, difficulty with the work, or something outside school. Talk to the student privately, set clear and consistent expectations, praise positive behaviour more often than you correct, and involve parents and school support when patterns persist.", "he's 9 and acts out when work gets hard", "That suggests avoidance. Break tasks into smaller steps, offer a choice (which problem first), and give him a quiet signal to ask for help without embarrassment. Check with the school's learning-support staff, because it's worth ruling out learning difficulties.")
d("expert_chat", "What's your view on microservices vs a monolith for a team of 4?", "For four people, start with a well-structured monolith. Microservices add operational overhead: deployment pipelines, networking, observability and distributed debugging. Keep modules separated cleanly so you can split a service out later if scaling or team size demands it.", "we have an ML component with different dependencies", "That's a reasonable reason to separate just that part: run the ML component as its own service with a narrow API, while keeping the rest as a monolith. It isolates heavy dependencies and lets you scale or deploy it independently without going all-in on microservices.")
d("coffee_chat", "why does my coffee taste bitter", "Common causes are water that's too hot, grinding too fine, over-extracting (brewing too long) or dark, old beans. Try slightly cooler water (around 92 to 96 C), a coarser grind or a shorter brew.", "I use a French press", "For a French press, use a coarse grind, about 4 minutes of steeping, and don't leave coffee sitting on the grounds after pouring. Decant into a mug or thermos, since extraction continues while it sits.")
d("coffee_chat", "I need to cut back on caffeine", "Taper gradually, for instance reducing by a cup every few days, to avoid withdrawal headaches. Swap some servings for half-caff or tea, drink water and avoid caffeine after early afternoon so your sleep improves.", "I have 5 cups a day", "Five cups is high (probably 400 mg or more). Go down to 4 for a few days, then 3, and so on. Replace the dropped cups with water, herbal tea or decaf to keep the habit, and expect mild tiredness or headaches for a few days.")
d("random_help", "How do I tie a tie?", "For a four-in-hand, the easiest: drape the tie with the wide end longer, cross the wide end over the narrow, wrap it around behind, bring it across the front again, pull it up through the neck loop and down through the front knot, then tighten. Adjust so the tip reaches your belt.")
d("random_help", "How do I change a flat tire?", "Park on level ground with the handbrake on and hazard lights on, loosen the wheel nuts slightly, jack up the car at the jacking point, remove the nuts and wheel, fit the spare, hand-tighten the nuts, lower the car and tighten in a star pattern. Check the spare's pressure and get the tyre repaired soon.")
d("random_help", "how can I fall asleep faster", "Keep a regular schedule, avoid screens and heavy meals before bed, keep your room cool and dark, and try slow breathing or a body scan. If you can't sleep after about 20 minutes, get up for a quiet activity and return when sleepy.")
d("random_help", "How do I remove a stuck jar lid?", "Run the lid under hot water for 30 seconds to expand the metal, or tap around the edge with a spoon. Use a rubber glove or band for grip. Pop the vacuum by sliding a spoon under the lid edge.")
d("random_help", "how do you politely tell someone they're talking too loud", "Use a light touch: lean in and say, \"Sorry, could you keep it down a little? I'm having trouble concentrating.\" Keeping it about your needs makes it less accusatory.")
d("random_help", "what do I do if I locked my keys in the car", "If the car is in a safe place, call roadside assistance, a locksmith or a spare-key holder. Do not break windows. If a child or pet is inside, or it's very hot, call emergency services right away.")
d("random_help", "How do I write a good LinkedIn headline?", "Include your role, your specialty and the value you offer: \"Data analyst | Turning messy sales data into clear decisions | SQL, Python, Tableau\". Avoid vague words like \"passionate\" and use terms recruiters search for.")
d("random_help", "How long should a resume be?", "One page for under about 10 years of experience, two pages for more. Put the most relevant material first, use bullet points that show results with numbers, and tailor it to each job.")
d("random_help", "how to sound confident in meetings", "Speak slowly, state conclusions first (\"I recommend X because Y\"), avoid hedges like \"just\" and \"maybe\", and prepare one point in advance so you can contribute early. Silence after you speak is fine.")
d("random_help", "What's the best way to learn to swim as an adult?", "Take lessons from a qualified instructor, since adult learners benefit from technique and a safe setting. Start with getting comfortable in water, floating and breathing, then kicking and arm strokes. Short, regular sessions help more than occasional long ones.")
d("random_help", "How do I read a nutrition label?", "Check the serving size first, because all values are per serving. Then look at calories, saturated fat, sugar, sodium and fibre. The ingredient list is ordered by weight, so what comes first is the most abundant.")
d("random_help", "What should I do if I get a call from the 'bank' asking for my PIN", "Hang up. Banks never ask for your PIN or full password by phone. Call the number on the back of your card or on the official website, and report the attempt. If you shared any details, contact your bank right away to block the card.")
d("random_help", "how do I deal with a bee in my house", "Stay calm and open a window or door. Bees usually fly toward light and will leave on their own. Avoid swatting. If it lands, a glass and a card can carry it outside.")
d("random_help", "what's the quickest way to learn the keyboard shortcuts for Windows", "Learn the few you'd use constantly: Ctrl+C/V/X/Z, Ctrl+F, Alt+Tab, Win+D, Win+L and Win+Shift+S for screenshots. Add one or two per week as you notice repeated actions.")
d("random_help", "how do I make a good first impression", "Be punctual, make eye contact, smile, listen more than you speak and remember their name. Curiosity about others tends to make a better impression than trying to impress.")
d("relationship_chat", "how do I know if I'm in love", "There's no single sign. Common ones are caring about their wellbeing as much as your own, feeling comfortable being yourself, wanting to share both small and big moments, and thinking of a future together. Infatuation is intense but quick; love tends to be steady and deepen with time.")
d("relationship_chat", "my partner and I keep arguing about chores", "That's one of the most common sources of conflict. Try listing all the tasks, including invisible ones like planning and remembering, then divide them by preference and time, not by who notices first. Review every few weeks. A calm weekly check-in beats arguing mid-chore.")
d("relationship_chat", "How do I get over jealousy?", "Notice what triggers it and what fear lies under it, such as being replaced or not being enough. Talk about it with your partner without accusations, and work on your own sense of worth. If it's intense or constant, a counsellor can help.")
d("relationship_chat", "Is long-distance dating worth it?", "It can be if both of you want it and there's a plan for closing the distance. Regular calls, scheduled visits and honest talk about the future help. Without an end date, many couples find it hard to sustain.")
d("relationship_chat", "How do I say 'I love you' for the first time?", "Say it when you feel it, privately and simply, without pressure on them to say it back: \"I wanted you to know I love you.\" Pick a calm moment, not mid-argument.")
d("parenting_chat", "How much screen time is OK for a 5 year old?", "Many paediatric guidelines suggest up to about one hour a day of good-quality content for ages 2 to 5, ideally watched together. Keep screens away from meals and bedtime, and make sure there's enough play, sleep and reading.")
d("parenting_chat", "My kid is afraid of the dark", "It's very common. Validate the fear, use a dim night light, check the room together for monsters if that helps, and build a calm bedtime routine. Gradually reduce the light over weeks. If the fear is intense or lasts long, talk to your paediatrician.")
d("parenting_chat", "how do I talk to my kid about death", "Use simple, honest words: \"Grandpa's body stopped working and he died. He won't come back.\" Avoid euphemisms like \"went to sleep.\" Let them ask questions, accept their feelings and answer again when they repeat.")
d("parenting_chat", "my 8 year old lies a lot", "At this age, lying often comes from fear of punishment or wishing to avoid trouble. Stay calm, praise truth-telling even when it's uncomfortable, and use natural consequences rather than harsh punishment. Avoid setting traps with questions you already know the answer to.")
d("finance2_chat", "what's the difference between a debit and a credit card", "A debit card takes money directly from your bank account. A credit card lets you borrow up to a limit and pay later; if you don't pay in full, you pay interest. Credit can build a credit history and sometimes offers protections, but it's easier to overspend.")
d("finance2_chat", "How do I read a pay slip?", "Look at gross pay (before deductions), then deductions such as income tax, social insurance or pension contributions, and net pay (what you receive). Check tax code or withholding details, hours and year-to-date totals. If something looks wrong, ask payroll.")
d("finance2_chat", "should I buy or lease a car?", "Buying costs more per month but you own the car afterwards, which is cheaper over a long time if you keep it for years. Leasing has lower payments and a newer car but mileage limits and no ownership. If you drive a lot or like to keep cars for a long time, buying usually wins.")
d("finance2_chat", "What is a good savings rate?", "A common target is 15 to 20% of gross income including retirement contributions, but start where you can. Even 5% builds the habit, and you can increase it with each raise.")
